{"authorityRole":"external_auditor","by":"buger","candidateKey":"","candidateKind":"","changeKey":"pr/9056","createdAt":"2026-10-02T21:31:48Z","decision":"accept_change","decisionDigest":"sha256:6387d014e039ac3b3fec6ca99985c0e550f380e63bf28fd7c76e113c0e68a883","decisionId":"rdec-87336b8ddd7ea47fd5cd00bba514a8f6","identitySource":"github_login","packageDigest":"sha256:e906aeb7213e5554b02dc382798dd624e19c3a81a4c671f786bd859557cfa8e2","packageIdentity":"epkg-8f9e54daedc02e4b34fa751963ef72b961221975bd8ab51a0c5d4a5542fd67ec","prNumber":"9056","reason":"Recommend accepting: a new summon now answers the earlier waiting caller as cancelled instead of leaving it stuck (#9057), and the head b7bd59c4 also fixes the burst race found on the first commit. On a live Omarchy session, a burst of 20 summons left 19 callers stuck on quattro c05d9019, 7 to 12 on the first commit 0b4fe29, and 0 on b7bd59c4, in all 6 runs. The two-summon case exits cleanly, and a real selection still returns its value. A headless regression test for overlapping requests fails on both older trees and passes 8/8 on this head. One gap remains outside this PR: if the menu process dies after a summon, the caller still waits forever (tracked on #9057).","repo":"https://github.com/omacom/omarchy","schemaVersion":"reqproof.review-decision.v1","scope":"change"}
